    historyContent: {
      origins: "Quadrille was introduced to the southern provinces of France around 1695 and shortly thereafter to Paris. It is an adaptation of the three-player Spanish game Ombre (Hombre) for four players. First English rules appeared in The Game of Quadrille; or Ombre by Four (1720), described as 'the favourite game at the French Court.'",
      originCountries: [
        'France',
        'Spain',
        'United Kingdom',
      ],
      timeline: [
        'ca. 1695: Quadrille introduced in southern France.',
        'Early 18th century: Spread to Paris and Britain; French rules published (1724).',
        '1720sâ€“1820s: Dominant card game at the French court and among British nobility; notably popular with women.',
        "1813: Referenced in Jane Austen's Pride and Prejudice (Lady Catherine de Bourgh).",
        'Mid-19th century: Declined in fashion, superseded by Whist and Boston.',
        'Present: Almost forgotten; still played in West Friesland and by enthusiasts.',
      ],
      evolution: 'Emerged as Ombre adapted for four players with variable partnerships (calling a King). Complex matador and ranking system preserved from Ombre. Influenced Boston Whist and Solo Whist. Mediateur (Mediator) was a later elaboration with additional bids.',
      cultural: "Was 'to the good taste of the French nation' and particularly favoured by women. Charles Lamb contrasted its 'chance-started, capricious, ever fluctuating alliances' with the steady antipathies of Whist. Frequently mentioned in 18thâ€“19th century literature and diaries.",
    },
    setupContent: {
      players: '4 players. Each plays for herself; temporary alliances form per deal via the called King.',
      deck: '40 cards: A, K, Q, J, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2 in each suit (8s, 9s, 10s removed).',
      equipment: '40-card pack, counters or chips for stakes, score sheet.',
      dealing: '1. Dealer shuffles; pone cuts. 2. Deal 10 cards each in batches of 4-3-3, 3-4-3, or 3-3-4. 3. Deal and play rotate right (counterclockwise).',
    },
